@@ -4,6 +4,23 @@
 supplies scoped native capture functions; the engine's ordinary view reducers
 consume their detached inputs. Request demand only edits a bounded memory queue.
 Each worker turn captures one selector, so a slow view cannot starve its peers.
+
+Pacing: the source watch fires on board and SQLite-sidecar
+writes, and each firing re-queued every wanted selector.
+The shared ``fact-board-capture`` worker repeatedly recaptured views. A
+deadline running from submission could expire while queued,
+making evidence stale and causing ``503 dependency_unavailable`` responses.
+A selector now starts at most once per ``min_gap_seconds`` (writes inside the gap coalesce into one capture; the
+legacy board snapshot refreshes at most every 20 s), and the deadline runs from the capture's own start. A
+board capture is 0.7-0.8 s alone but 4-8 s cold and longer under the backend's GIL contention, so the owner's 10 s
+budget failed every one there (failed even when it finished: ``_build`` re-checks the time); a board capture
+now has ``max_capture_seconds`` (30 s), below the 60 s validity, so a slow capture still lands as fresh evidence.
+
+Every ``ViewOwner`` uses this source, so the project tree's views (``fact_tree_startup``) are paced and limited the
+same way. A capture queued behind a wedged shared worker reports no failure: its evidence goes stale when its 60 s
+validity expires (``fact_expiry``) instead of within the owner's 10 s budget. The next refresh is timed from the
+last evidence's observation, not its commit: one selector whose captures finish within 30 s keeps its validity;
+in a round of several views, earlier views' evidence is older by the later captures' durations.
 """
